{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786606689237,"sessionID":"ses_005f19fdfffeOBUKa90a14PLKH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_gGB3XmWMbwGeqFDN8Nvu1424","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0144-system-deep-loop-036-p0-remediation/specs/system-deep-loop/036-deep-loop-innovation/057-phase-consolidation-research/research/lineages/ds-a/iterations/iteration-004.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0144-system-deep-loop-036-p0-remediation/specs/system-deep-loop/036-deep-loop-innovation/057-phase-consolidation-research/research/lineages/ds-a/iterations/iteration-004.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Iteration 4: timeline.md Design — Chronological Lineage That Survives Renumbering\n2: \n3: ## Focus\n4: Design the `timeline.md` record so the \"which spec folder was worked on first and which came after\" lineage is durable across any renumbering. Derive the canonical order from git first-add timestamps + graph-metadata created_at, and specify schema + placement.\n5: \n6: ## Findings\n7: \n8: ### 1. Two timestamp sources exist and partially disagree\n9: - **Git first-add** (`git log --all --follow --diff-filter=A` on each folder's spec.md, surviving the `.opencode/specs → specs/` flip at `606e55cb8a9`): 001 on 07-14; a big batch of 002-017 on 07-15/16; 018 on 07-20; 050 on 07-27; 019/020 on 07-29; 021 on 08-03; 022-032 on 07-31; 033 on 08-08; 035 on 08-07; 047-049 on 08-08; 051 on 08-12; 052 on 08-12; 053-056 on 08-13.\n10: - **graph-metadata `derived.created_at`**: 001/002 on 07-15; 003-017 on 07-16; 018 on 07-19; 050 on 07-27; 019/020 on 07-29; 021 on 07-30; 022-032 on 07-31; 033 on 08-05; 035 on 08-07; 047-049 on 08-08; 051 on 08-12; 052 on 08-12; 053-056 on 08-13.\n11: - Discrepancies: 021 git=08-03 vs gm=07-30; 033 git=08-08 vs gm=08-05; 018 git=07-20 vs gm=07-19. Because the whole 003-017 planning batch was committed nearly simultaneously (07-15/16) and graph-metadata created_at was written during planning (before execution), **neither source alone is a reliable \"worked on\" order** — git first-add reflects file introduction; created_at reflects spec creation. [SOURCE: git log + graph-metadata, see iteration-001.md]\n12: \n13: ### 2. Authoritative worked-order signal: `derived.last_save_at` + status\n14: - `graph-metadata.json` `derived.last_save_at` is the best \"actively worked\" timestamp (continuity save events). Example spread confirms the remediation/execution wave: 016 saved 08-12, 017 saved 08-12, 051 saved 08-12 18:43, 052 saved 08-12 21:11, 053-056 saved 08-13 06:48 — i.e., execution order does NOT match folder number in the later wave (047-049 created before 050 but 050 saved later; 051-056 are newest). [SOURCE: graph-metadata last_save_at per child]\n15: - But `last_save_at` can be backfilled by `generate-context.js` runs that touch the whole tree (many share identical save timestamps, e.g., 007-014 all saved 07-17 13:21), so it is a *bounding* signal, not a strict total order.\n16: \n17: ### 3. timeline.md design (concrete)\n18: \n19: **Purpose:** a human+tool readable, append-only record of the true chronological order each spec folder was created and worked on, keyed by a stable identity that is NOT the folder number.\n20: \n21: **Location:**\n22: - Root: `specs/system-deep-loop/036-deep-loop-innovation/timeline.md` (the canonical lineage record).\n23: - Per group parent (after consolidation): each new group parent gets a `timeline.md` scoped to its children. The root one remains canonical.\n24: \n25: **Schema (markdown table, machine-parseable):**\n26: \n27: ```markdown\n28: # Timeline — 036-deep-loop-innovation (chronological lineage record)\n29: > Immutable identity record. Do NOT reorder rows; append only. Renumbering folders MUST NOT change this file.\n30: \n31: | chrono_id | folder_slug | band | created_at | git_first_add | last_save_at | status | worked_order |\n32: |-----------|-------------|------|------------|---------------|--------------|--------|--------------|\n33: | T001      | 001-deep-loop-market-research | 000-foundation-and-planning | 2026-07-15T19:00:45Z | 2026-07-14T22:32:08+02 | 2026-07-17T13:31:05Z | complete | 1 |\n34: | T002      | 002-deep-loop-effectiveness-and-fanout | 000-foundation-and-planning | 2026-07-15T19:00:46Z | 2026-07-15T18:45:50+02 | 2026-07-17T13:31:05Z | complete | 2 |\n35: ...\n36: ```\n37: \n38: **Rules:**\n39: 1. **`chrono_id`** is the stable key (T001, T002, …) assigned once in the ORIGINAL folder order (git first-add date, tie-broken by folder number for the planning batch). Survives any renumber; it is the join key for migration.\n40: 2. **`worked_order`** is the derived execution sequence: git_first_add as primary, created_at as tie-break, last_save_at to break remaining ties; a `notes` column records anomalies (e.g., \"part of 07-15/16 planning batch\", \"repaired/redone in remediation wave\").\n41: 3. **`band`** column records the new group-parent after consolidation so the timeline doubles as a mapping table.\n42: 4. **Append-only invariant:** a new phase child appended at the END with the next chrono_id; never reorder existing rows. Renumbering a folder updates ONLY its `folder_slug`/`band` cells, never `chrono_id`/`worked_order`/created timestamps.\n43: 5. **Anti-drift:** the file is generated once (from git log + graph-metadata) and then reviewed; it is a *source document* for the migration mapping (iteration-3 table) so a renumber cannot lose the original order.\n44: 6. **Tooling:** a small generator could emit it, but the design requirement is that it exists and is append-only before any rename executes.\n45: \n46: ### 4. Worked-order (chrono_id) for all 44 children (derived)\n47: 1. 001 (07-14) — truly first\n48: 2. 002-017 planning batch (07-15/16; order 002→003→004→005→006→007→008→009→010→011→012→013→014→015→016→017 by folder number)\n49: 3. 018 (07-19/20)\n50: 4. 050 (07-27) — note: 050 was authored EARLY (07-27) despite its 05x number (renumbered later into the defect wave)\n51: 5. 019, 020 (07-29)\n52: 6. 022-032 (07-31; order 022→023→024→025→026→027→028→029→030→031→032)\n53: 7. 021 (07-30/08-03) — created with the 022-032 batch but first committed 08-03\n54: 8. 033 (08-05/08-08)\n55: 9. 035 (08-07)\n56: 10. 047, 048, 049 (08-08)\n57: 11. 051 (08-12)\n58: 12. 052 (08-12)\n59: 13. 053, 054, 055, 056 (08-13)\n60: \n61: This is exactly the ordering a renumbering would destroy (050 before 019/020; 021 after 022-032; 047-049 after 033; 051-056 newest). The numbering DOES NOT reflect work order in the 02x-05x range — a key argument for the timeline record.\n62: \n63: ### 5. Where the migration plan consumes timeline.md\n64: - The iteration-3 mapping table (old→new) must be cross-checked against timeline.md `chrono_id` so nothing is dropped or duplicated.\n65: - After consolidation, the root `timeline.md` and each group-parent `timeline.md` are the only durable place the pre-renumber order survives.\n66: \n67: ## Assessment\n68: - newInfoRatio: 0.85\n69: - Novelty justification: Established the two-source discrepancy, selected last_save_at as the work-bound signal, defined the timeline.md schema/rules/placement, and produced the full 44-folder worked-order table that renumbering would destroy. Directly answers KQ4.\n70: - Confidence: High (derived from git + graph-metadata; the exact tie-breaks are documented as rules for the generator).\n71: \n72: ## Reflection\n73: - What worked: cross-referencing three timestamp sources exposed that numbering ≠ work order in the 02x-05x range.\n74: - What failed / ruled out: relying on folder number or either timestamp source alone as the order.\n75: \n76: ## Recommended Next Focus\n77: Iteration 5: Feasibility verdict (KQ1) consolidation: consolidate all findings into the final recommendation — is it worth it, which shape, what must be true to do it safely — and surface open risks (map-status drift for 053-056, phase-tree.json 17-vs-44, memory re-ingest ordering).\n\n(End of file - total 77 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Iteration 4: timeline.md Design — Chronological Lineage That Survives Renumbering\n\n## Focus\nDesign the `timeline.md` record so the \"which spec folder was worked on first and which came after\" lineage is durable across any renumbering. Derive the canonical order from git first-add timestamps + graph-metadata created_at, and specify schema + placement.\n\n## Findings\n\n### 1. Two timestamp sources exist and partially disagree\n- **Git first-add** (`git log --all --follow --diff-filter=A` on each folder's spec.md, surviving the `.opencode/specs → specs/` flip at `606e55cb8a9`): 001 on 07-14; a big batch of 002-017 on 07-15/16; 018 on 07-20; 050 on 07-27; 019/020 on 07-29; 021 on 08-03; 022-032 on 07-31; 033 on 08-08; 035 on 08-07; 047-049 on 08-08; 051 on 08-12; 052 on 08-12; 053-056 on 08-13.\n- **graph-metadata `derived.created_at`**: 001/002 on 07-15; 003-017 on 07-16; 018 on 07-19; 050 on 07-27; 019/020 on 07-29; 021 on 07-30; 022-032 on 07-31; 033 on 08-05; 035 on 08-07; 047-049 on 08-08; 051 on 08-12; 052 on 08-12; 053-056 on 08-13.\n- Discrepancies: 021 git=08-03 vs gm=07-30; 033 git=08-08 vs gm=08-05; 018 git=07-20 vs gm=07-19. Because the whole 003-017 planning batch was committed nearly simultaneously (07-15/16) and graph-metadata created_at was written during planning (before execution), **neither source alone is a reliable \"worked on\" order** — git first-add reflects file introduction; created_at reflects spec creation. [SOURCE: git log + graph-metadata, see iteration-001.md]\n\n### 2. Authoritative worked-order signal: `derived.last_save_at` + status\n- `graph-metadata.json` `derived.last_save_at` is the best \"actively worked\" timestamp (continuity save events). Example spread confirms the remediation/execution wave: 016 saved 08-12, 017 saved 08-12, 051 saved 08-12 18:43, 052 saved 08-12 21:11, 053-056 saved 08-13 06:48 — i.e., execution order does NOT match folder number in the later wave (047-049 created before 050 but 050 saved later; 051-056 are newest). [SOURCE: graph-metadata last_save_at per child]\n- But `last_save_at` can be backfilled by `generate-context.js` runs that touch the whole tree (many share identical save timestamps, e.g., 007-014 all saved 07-17 13:21), so it is a *bounding* signal, not a strict total order.\n\n### 3. timeline.md design (concrete)\n\n**Purpose:** a human+tool readable, append-only record of the true chronological order each spec folder was created and worked on, keyed by a stable identity that is NOT the folder number.\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0144-system-deep-loop-036-p0-remediation/specs/system-deep-loop/036-deep-loop-innovation/057-phase-consolidation-research/research/lineages/ds-a/iterations/iteration-004.md","text":"# Iteration 4: timeline.md Design — Chronological Lineage That Survives Renumbering\n\n## Focus\nDesign the `timeline.md` record so the \"which spec folder was worked on first and which came after\" lineage is durable across any renumbering. Derive the canonical order from git first-add timestamps + graph-metadata created_at, and specify schema + placement.\n\n## Findings\n\n### 1. Two timestamp sources exist and partially disagree\n- **Git first-add** (`git log --all --follow --diff-filter=A` on each folder's spec.md, surviving the `.opencode/specs → specs/` flip at `606e55cb8a9`): 001 on 07-14; a big batch of 002-017 on 07-15/16; 018 on 07-20; 050 on 07-27; 019/020 on 07-29; 021 on 08-03; 022-032 on 07-31; 033 on 08-08; 035 on 08-07; 047-049 on 08-08; 051 on 08-12; 052 on 08-12; 053-056 on 08-13.\n- **graph-metadata `derived.created_at`**: 001/002 on 07-15; 003-017 on 07-16; 018 on 07-19; 050 on 07-27; 019/020 on 07-29; 021 on 07-30; 022-032 on 07-31; 033 on 08-05; 035 on 08-07; 047-049 on 08-08; 051 on 08-12; 052 on 08-12; 053-056 on 08-13.\n- Discrepancies: 021 git=08-03 vs gm=07-30; 033 git=08-08 vs gm=08-05; 018 git=07-20 vs gm=07-19. Because the whole 003-017 planning batch was committed nearly simultaneously (07-15/16) and graph-metadata created_at was written during planning (before execution), **neither source alone is a reliable \"worked on\" order** — git first-add reflects file introduction; created_at reflects spec creation. [SOURCE: git log + graph-metadata, see iteration-001.md]\n\n### 2. Authoritative worked-order signal: `derived.last_save_at` + status\n- `graph-metadata.json` `derived.last_save_at` is the best \"actively worked\" timestamp (continuity save events). Example spread confirms the remediation/execution wave: 016 saved 08-12, 017 saved 08-12, 051 saved 08-12 18:43, 052 saved 08-12 21:11, 053-056 saved 08-13 06:48 — i.e., execution order does NOT match folder number in the later wave (047-049 created before 050 but 050 saved later; 051-056 are newest). [SOURCE: graph-metadata last_save_at per child]\n- But `last_save_at` can be backfilled by `generate-context.js` runs that touch the whole tree (many share identical save timestamps, e.g., 007-014 all saved 07-17 13:21), so it is a *bounding* signal, not a strict total order.\n\n### 3. timeline.md design (concrete)\n\n**Purpose:** a human+tool readable, append-only record of the true chronological order each spec folder was created and worked on, keyed by a stable identity that is NOT the folder number.\n\n**Location:**\n- Root: `specs/system-deep-loop/036-deep-loop-innovation/timeline.md` (the canonical lineage record).\n- Per group parent (after consolidation): each new group parent gets a `timeline.md` scoped to its children. The root one remains canonical.\n\n**Schema (markdown table, machine-parseable):**\n\n```markdown\n# Timeline — 036-deep-loop-innovation (chronological lineage record)\n> Immutable identity record. Do NOT reorder rows; append only. Renumbering folders MUST NOT change this file.\n\n| chrono_id | folder_slug | band | created_at | git_first_add | last_save_at | status | worked_order |\n|-----------|-------------|------|------------|---------------|--------------|--------|--------------|\n| T001      | 001-deep-loop-market-research | 000-foundation-and-planning | 2026-07-15T19:00:45Z | 2026-07-14T22:32:08+02 | 2026-07-17T13:31:05Z | complete | 1 |\n| T002      | 002-deep-loop-effectiveness-and-fanout | 000-foundation-and-planning | 2026-07-15T19:00:46Z | 2026-07-15T18:45:50+02 | 2026-07-17T13:31:05Z | complete | 2 |\n...\n```\n\n**Rules:**\n1. **`chrono_id`** is the stable key (T001, T002, …) assigned once in the ORIGINAL folder order (git first-add date, tie-broken by folder number for the planning batch). Survives any renumber; it is the join key for migration.\n2. **`worked_order`** is the derived execution sequence: git_first_add as primary, created_at as tie-break, last_save_at to break remaining ties; a `notes` column records anomalies (e.g., \"part of 07-15/16 planning batch\", \"repaired/redone in remediation wave\").\n3. **`band`** column records the new group-parent after consolidation so the timeline doubles as a mapping table.\n4. **Append-only invariant:** a new phase child appended at the END with the next chrono_id; never reorder existing rows. Renumbering a folder updates ONLY its `folder_slug`/`band` cells, never `chrono_id`/`worked_order`/created timestamps.\n5. **Anti-drift:** the file is generated once (from git log + graph-metadata) and then reviewed; it is a *source document* for the migration mapping (iteration-3 table) so a renumber cannot lose the original order.\n6. **Tooling:** a small generator could emit it, but the design requirement is that it exists and is append-only before any rename executes.\n\n### 4. Worked-order (chrono_id) for all 44 children (derived)\n1. 001 (07-14) — truly first\n2. 002-017 planning batch (07-15/16; order 002→003→004→005→006→007→008→009→010→011→012→013→014→015→016→017 by folder number)\n3. 018 (07-19/20)\n4. 050 (07-27) — note: 050 was authored EARLY (07-27) despite its 05x number (renumbered later into the defect wave)\n5. 019, 020 (07-29)\n6. 022-032 (07-31; order 022→023→024→025→026→027→028→029→030→031→032)\n7. 021 (07-30/08-03) — created with the 022-032 batch but first committed 08-03\n8. 033 (08-05/08-08)\n9. 035 (08-07)\n10. 047, 048, 049 (08-08)\n11. 051 (08-12)\n12. 052 (08-12)\n13. 053, 054, 055, 056 (08-13)\n\nThis is exactly the ordering a renumbering would destroy (050 before 019/020; 021 after 022-032; 047-049 after 033; 051-056 newest). The numbering DOES NOT reflect work order in the 02x-05x range — a key argument for the timeline record.\n\n### 5. Where the migration plan consumes timeline.md\n- The iteration-3 mapping table (old→new) must be cross-checked against timeline.md `chrono_id` so nothing is dropped or duplicated.\n- After consolidation, the root `timeline.md` and each group-parent `timeline.md` are the only durable place the pre-renumber order survives.\n\n## Assessment\n- newInfoRatio: 0.85\n- Novelty justification: Established the two-source discrepancy, selected last_save_at as the work-bound signal, defined the timeline.md schema/rules/placement, and produced the full 44-folder worked-order table that renumbering would destroy. Directly answers KQ4.\n- Confidence: High (derived from git + graph-metadata; the exact tie-breaks are documented as rules for the generator).\n\n## Reflection\n- What worked: cross-referencing three timestamp sources exposed that numbering ≠ work order in the 02x-05x range.\n- What failed / ruled out: relying on folder number or either timestamp source alone as the order.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\nIteration 5: Feasibility verdict (KQ1) consolidation: consolidate all findings into the final recommendation — is it worth it, which shape, what must be true to do it safely — and surface open risks (map-status drift for 053-056, phase-tree.json 17-vs-44, memory re-ingest ordering).","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":77,"totalLines":77,"truncated":false}},"title":"specs/system-deep-loop/036-deep-loop-innovation/057-phase-consolidation-research/research/lineages/ds-a/iterations/iteration-004.md","time":{"start":1786606689190,"end":1786606689235}},"id":"prt_ffa0e9a04001i7QLwMczrLCcYi","sessionID":"ses_005f19fdfffeOBUKa90a14PLKH","messageID":"msg_ffa0e86c6001X5X9xCt0Q9wIlI"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786606689558,"sessionID":"ses_005f19fdfffeOBUKa90a14PLKH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01_j79xTWWYhoI5VC1MvtAG0751","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0144-system-deep-loop-036-p0-remediation/specs/system-deep-loop/036-deep-loop-innovation/057-phase-consolidation-research/research/lineages/ds-a/findings-registry.json"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0144-system-deep-loop-036-p0-remediation/specs/system-deep-loop/036-deep-loop-innovation/057-phase-consolidation-research/research/lineages/ds-a/findings-registry.json</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: {\n2:   \"openQuestions\": [],\n3:   \"resolvedQuestions\": [\n4:     {\"id\": \"KQ1\", \"text\": \"Is consolidation into fewer larger multi-phase parents feasible and beneficial for context optimization?\", \"answer\": \"Yes — feasible (spec-kit supports arbitrary phase-parent depth); beneficial for dependency-spine groups (010-substrate-and-orchestration, 020-mode-migration-and-cutover), marginal for independent leaves; safe only with timeline-first disciplined migration.\"},\n5:     {\"id\": \"KQ2\", \"text\": \"Which child folders cluster into which multi-phase parents (by theme/dependency, optimized names)?\", \"answer\": \"7-group shape: 000-foundation-and-planning (001-004), 010-substrate-and-orchestration (005-011), 020-mode-migration-and-cutover (012-015), 030-gate-closeout-and-drift (016-018), 040-runtime-hygiene-and-remediation (019-032), 050-hardening-and-repair (033,035,047-052), 060-review-and-conformance (053-056). Full 44-row mapping in research.md/iteration-003.\"},\n6:     {\"id\": \"KQ3\", \"text\": \"What is the full migration plan (renames + ALL reference/JSON updates)?\", \"answer\": \"15-surface checklist: parent+child graph-metadata, spec.md phase maps, adjacency lines, description.json, phase-tree.json, validate.sh embedded manifest + recomputed sha256, recursive-child-manifest.vitest.ts, specs/descriptions.json, 98 cross-repo files (both specs/ and .opencode/specs/ spellings), memory re-ingest, handover/docs. Execution order defined.\"},\n7:     {\"id\": \"KQ4\", \"text\": \"How should timeline.md be designed so chronological lineage survives renumbering?\", \"answer\": \"Append-only table with stable chrono_id (T001...), folder_slug, band, created_at, git_first_add, last_save_at, status, worked_order. Generated from git first-add + graph-metadata before any rename; renames update only slug/band cells.\"}\n8:   ],\n9:   \"keyFindings\": [\n10:     \"44 direct children, in sync with parent children_ids (no orphans)\",\n11:     \"13 children are already phase parents; 31 are leaves\",\n12:     \"validate.sh hardcodes a 40-child manifest for 036 with embedded sha256 f6cf1e94... (053-056 missing; passes only due to SPECKIT_CHILD_MANIFEST_FILE gating)\",\n13:     \"Parent spec.md phase map statuses stale for 053-056\",\n14:     \"manifest/phase-tree.json covers only 001-017 despite declaring 44 children\",\n15:     \"98 non-036 files reference 036-deep-loop-innovation, incl. legacy .opencode/specs/ spellings in runtime code/tests\",\n16:     \"Numbering does not match work order in the 02x-05x range (050 before 019/020; 021 after 022-032)\"\n17:   ],\n18:   \"ruledOutDirections\": [\n19:     {\"approach\": \"Flat 5-group split treating all 44 as equal leaves\", \"reason\": \"13 children already phase parents; remediation wave not a dependency spine\"},\n20:     {\"approach\": \"Flat renumber with no group parents\", \"reason\": \"No context-optimization gain at equal cost\"},\n21:     {\"approach\": \"Single flat consolidation of all 44 into one level\", \"reason\": \"Zero benefit, same migration cost\"},\n22:     {\"approach\": \"Folder number as chronological order\", \"reason\": \"Numbering diverges from work order in 02x-05x\"}\n23:   ],\n24:   \"coverageBySources\": {\n25:     \"on-disk census\": 44,\n26:     \"git log first-add\": 44,\n27:     \"child graph-metadata\": 44,\n28:     \"validate.sh\": 1,\n29:     \"vitest fixtures\": 1,\n30:     \"descriptions.json\": 133,\n31:     \"cross-repo refs\": 98\n32:   },\n33:   \"convergenceScore\": 0.81,\n34:   \"blockedStopHistory\": [],\n35:   \"graphConvergenceScore\": null,\n36:   \"graphDecision\": null,\n37:   \"graphBlockers\": [],\n38:   \"lastUpdated\": \"2026-08-13T09:30:00Z\"\n39: }\n\n(End of file - total 39 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\n  \"openQuestions\": [],\n  \"resolvedQuestions\": [\n    {\"id\": \"KQ1\", \"text\": \"Is consolidation into fewer larger multi-phase parents feasible and beneficial for context optimization?\", \"answer\": \"Yes — feasible (spec-kit supports arbitrary phase-parent depth); beneficial for dependency-spine groups (010-substrate-and-orchestration, 020-mode-migration-and-cutover), marginal for independent leaves; safe only with timeline-first disciplined migration.\"},\n    {\"id\": \"KQ2\", \"text\": \"Which child folders cluster into which multi-phase parents (by theme/dependency, optimized names)?\", \"answer\": \"7-group shape: 000-foundation-and-planning (001-004), 010-substrate-and-orchestration (005-011), 020-mode-migration-and-cutover (012-015), 030-gate-closeout-and-drift (016-018), 040-runtime-hygiene-and-remediation (019-032), 050-hardening-and-repair (033,035,047-052), 060-review-and-conformance (053-056). Full 44-row mapping in research.md/iteration-003.\"},\n    {\"id\": \"KQ3\", \"text\": \"What is the full migration plan (renames + ALL reference/JSON updates)?\", \"answer\": \"15-surface checklist: parent+child graph-metadata, spec.md phase maps, adjacency lines, description.json, phase-tree.json, validate.sh embedded manifest + recomputed sha256, recursive-child-manifest.vitest.ts, specs/descriptions.json, 98 cross-repo files (both specs/ and .opencode/specs/ spellings), memory re-ingest, handover/docs. Execution order defined.\"},\n    {\"id\": \"KQ4\", \"text\": \"How should timeline.md be designed so chronological lineage survives renumbering?\", \"answer\": \"Append-only table with stable chrono_id (T001...), folder_slug, band, created_at, git_first_add, last_save_at, status, worked_order. Generated from git first-add + graph-metadata before any rename; renames update only slug/band cells.\"}\n  ],\n  \"keyFindings\": [\n    \"44 direct children, in sync with parent children_ids (no orphans)\",\n    \"13 children are already phase parents; 31 are leaves\",\n    \"validate.sh hardcodes a 40-child manifest for 036 with embedded sha256 f6cf1e94... (053-056 missing; passes only due to SPECKIT_CHILD_MANIFEST_FILE gating)\",\n    \"Parent spec.md phase map statuses stale for 053-056\",\n    \"manifest/phase-tree.json covers only 001-017 despite declaring 44 children\",\n    \"98 non-036 files reference 036-deep-loop-innovation, incl. legacy .opencode/specs/ spellings in runtime code/tests\",\n    \"Numbering does not match work order in the 02x-05x range (050 before 019/020; 021 after 022-032)\"\n  ],\n  \"ruledOutDirections\": [\n    {\"approach\": \"Flat 5-group split treating all 44 as equal leaves\", \"reason\": \"13 children already phase parents; remediation wave not a dependency spine\"},\n    {\"approach\": \"Flat renumber with no group parents\", \"reason\": \"No context-optimization gain at equal cost\"},","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0144-system-deep-loop-036-p0-remediation/specs/system-deep-loop/036-deep-loop-innovation/057-phase-consolidation-research/research/lineages/ds-a/findings-registry.json","text":"{\n  \"openQuestions\": [],\n  \"resolvedQuestions\": [\n    {\"id\": \"KQ1\", \"text\": \"Is consolidation into fewer larger multi-phase parents feasible and beneficial for context optimization?\", \"answer\": \"Yes — feasible (spec-kit supports arbitrary phase-parent depth); beneficial for dependency-spine groups (010-substrate-and-orchestration, 020-mode-migration-and-cutover), marginal for independent leaves; safe only with timeline-first disciplined migration.\"},\n    {\"id\": \"KQ2\", \"text\": \"Which child folders cluster into which multi-phase parents (by theme/dependency, optimized names)?\", \"answer\": \"7-group shape: 000-foundation-and-planning (001-004), 010-substrate-and-orchestration (005-011), 020-mode-migration-and-cutover (012-015), 030-gate-closeout-and-drift (016-018), 040-runtime-hygiene-and-remediation (019-032), 050-hardening-and-repair (033,035,047-052), 060-review-and-conformance (053-056). Full 44-row mapping in research.md/iteration-003.\"},\n    {\"id\": \"KQ3\", \"text\": \"What is the full migration plan (renames + ALL reference/JSON updates)?\", \"answer\": \"15-surface checklist: parent+child graph-metadata, spec.md phase maps, adjacency lines, description.json, phase-tree.json, validate.sh embedded manifest + recomputed sha256, recursive-child-manifest.vitest.ts, specs/descriptions.json, 98 cross-repo files (both specs/ and .opencode/specs/ spellings), memory re-ingest, handover/docs. Execution order defined.\"},\n    {\"id\": \"KQ4\", \"text\": \"How should timeline.md be designed so chronological lineage survives renumbering?\", \"answer\": \"Append-only table with stable chrono_id (T001...), folder_slug, band, created_at, git_first_add, last_save_at, status, worked_order. Generated from git first-add + graph-metadata before any rename; renames update only slug/band cells.\"}\n  ],\n  \"keyFindings\": [\n    \"44 direct children, in sync with parent children_ids (no orphans)\",\n    \"13 children are already phase parents; 31 are leaves\",\n    \"validate.sh hardcodes a 40-child manifest for 036 with embedded sha256 f6cf1e94... (053-056 missing; passes only due to SPECKIT_CHILD_MANIFEST_FILE gating)\",\n    \"Parent spec.md phase map statuses stale for 053-056\",\n    \"manifest/phase-tree.json covers only 001-017 despite declaring 44 children\",\n    \"98 non-036 files reference 036-deep-loop-innovation, incl. legacy .opencode/specs/ spellings in runtime code/tests\",\n    \"Numbering does not match work order in the 02x-05x range (050 before 019/020; 021 after 022-032)\"\n  ],\n  \"ruledOutDirections\": [\n    {\"approach\": \"Flat 5-group split treating all 44 as equal leaves\", \"reason\": \"13 children already phase parents; remediation wave not a dependency spine\"},\n    {\"approach\": \"Flat renumber with no group parents\", \"reason\": \"No context-optimization gain at equal cost\"},\n    {\"approach\": \"Single flat consolidation of all 44 into one level\", \"reason\": \"Zero benefit, same migration cost\"},\n    {\"approach\": \"Folder number as chronological order\", \"reason\": \"Numbering diverges from work order in 02x-05x\"}\n  ],\n  \"coverageBySources\": {\n    \"on-disk census\": 44,\n    \"git log first-add\": 44,\n    \"child graph-metadata\": 44,\n    \"validate.sh\": 1,\n    \"vitest fixtures\": 1,\n    \"descriptions.json\": 133,\n    \"cross-repo refs\": 98\n  },\n  \"convergenceScore\": 0.81,\n  \"blockedStopHistory\": [],\n  \"graphConvergenceScore\": null,\n  \"graphDecision\": null,\n  \"graphBlockers\": [],\n  \"lastUpdated\": \"2026-08-13T09:30:00Z\"\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":39,"totalLines":39,"truncated":false}},"title":"specs/system-deep-loop/036-deep-loop-innovation/057-phase-consolidation-research/research/lineages/ds-a/findings-registry.json","time":{"start":1786606689552,"end":1786606689557}},"id":"prt_ffa0e9bc2001vdzrrQSW5lAmPs","sessionID":"ses_005f19fdfffeOBUKa90a14PLKH","messageID":"msg_ffa0e86c6001X5X9xCt0Q9wIlI"}}
